From 33e4bb974d60060a549b03d89ecf1adceafb6426 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 04:48:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] =?UTF-8?q?chromia(import):=20header-name=20columns,?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20Remarks=E2=86=92QC=20grade,=20delete=20imported=20file?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The register import now reads columns by HEADER NAME rather than fixed position,
so the six required columns — Production Date, Batch Number, Slab Number, Base
Material / Slab Name, File Name / Planned Design, Remarks — are found in any
order, under minor name variations (Batch No. / Slab Batch Number, Program /
File Name, Date / Production Date...), with extra columns ignored. Thickness and
In-time are optional. A missing required column is named in the error instead of
a bare FAILED, and a good run says "Import Successful". The legacy monthly
register still imports through its own positional parser, auto-detected.

From the remark, QC is completed for historical rows: Dispatch → Grade A,
Stock → Grade A, Sample Cutting → Grade B, Recalibration → Grade C, each written
as a real grade decision (matching the intake QC path), so imported rows carry a
genuine Grade + Outcome, not just a disposition.

The import history gains a Delete button that removes an imported file and every
slab it brought in — through the existing single-slab delete cascade, with an
audit entry and the slab numbers freed. Existing duplicate-slab validation and
the persistence path are unchanged. Tests cover header detection, name
variations, missing-column reporting, optional columns and the remark→grade map.
